{"records":[{"id":"74pvb35ggj","title":"RNA-seq in chilling-treated NIP, 93-11 and CSSL line","description":"The archive contains the gene expression matrix from \"Integrated global analysis reveals vitamin E-vitamin K1 sub-network, downstream of COLD1, underlying rice chilling tolerance divergence\" by Luo et al. A complete archive of raw sequencing data from this study is accessible from the Genome Sequence Archive in National Genomics Data Center, Beijing Institute of Genomics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, under accession number CRA003197.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-12T08:20:21.603Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/74pvb35ggj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/74pvb35ggj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jingyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dt676f75fz","title":"Synergistic Targeting of Pyroptosis and cfDNA by 4-Octyl itaconate-Loaded ROS-Responsive Nanoparticles Attenuates Inflammatory Diseases","description":"Pyroptosis-induced release of inflammatory factors and abnormally elevated levels of cell-free DNA (cfDNA) in inflammatory tissues are key drivers of inflammatory disease progression. Macrophages play a pivotal role in coordinating inflammatory immune responses, making their activity modulation crucial for treating inflammatory diseases. 4-Octyl itaconate (4-OI) exerts immunomodulatory effects by inhibiting macrophage pyroptosis, thereby reducing oxidative stress and inflammatory responses. Additionally, cationic nanoparticles demonstrate significant therapeutic advantages in inflammatory diseases due to their high affinity for cfDNA. Herein, we propose a combined therapeutic strategy employing ROS-responsive cationic nanoparticles to capture cfDNA while utilizing 4-OI release to reduce pyroptosis in inflammatory tissues, thereby remodeling the inflammatory microenvironment. This approach demonstrated excellent biocompatibility and efficacy in mouse models of inflammatory bowel diseases (including ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease) and sepsis. The study reveals that this regimen exhibits low toxicity and substantial therapeutic potential, offering a novel treatment option for inflammatory diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-21T15:59:43.17Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dt676f75fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dt676f75fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hery","last_name":"Peng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmaceutics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"829ywfmvrn","title":"Cofilactin rod formation mediates inflammation-induced neurite degeneration.","description":"Uruk et al. demonstrate that the brain inflammatory response causes neurite (axon and dendrite) degeneration by a mechanism involving NOX2-induced cofilactin rod (CAR) formation. Neurites are more vulnerable to Inflammation-induced injury than neuronal cell bodies, and neurite loss in the absence of neuronal cell body loss is associated with functional impairment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-04T15:21:19.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/829ywfmvrn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/829ywfmvrn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gökhan","last_name":"Uruk","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05rsv9s98","name":"United States Department of Veterans Affairs","grant_id":"RAS-1IO1 BX004884"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"JRB-1R01AG044812"}],"categories":["Inflammation","Nerve Degeneration"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hf233jr6tr","title":"SARS-CoV-2 infection triggers profibrotic macrophage responses and lung fibrosis | Wendisch, Dietrich, Mari, von Stillfried et al. ","description":"COVID-19-induced ‘acute respiratory distress syndrome’ (ARDS) is associated with prolonged respiratory failure and high mortality, but the mechanistic basis of lung injury remains incompletely understood. Here, we analyzed pulmonary immune responses and lung pathology in two cohorts of patients with COVID-19 ARDS using functional single cell genomics, immunohistology and electron microscopy. We describe an accumulation of CD163-expressing monocyte-derived macrophages that acquired a profibrotic transcriptional phenotype during COVID-19 ARDS. Gene set enrichment and computational data integration revealed a significant similarity between COVID-19-associated macrophages and profibrotic macrophage populations identified in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. COVID-19 ARDS was associated with clinical, radiographic, histopathological, and ultrastructural hallmarks of pulmonary fibrosis. Exposure of human monocytes to SARS-CoV-2, but not Influenza A virus or viral RNA analogs, was sufficient to induce a similar profibrotic phenotype in vitro. In conclusion, we demonstrate that SARS-CoV-2 triggers profibrotic macrophage responses and pronounced fibroproliferative ARDS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-22T14:22:43.981Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hf233jr6tr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hf233jr6tr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Antoine-Emmanuel","last_name":"Saliba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Leif Erik","last_name":"Sander","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel ","last_name":"Wendisch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Oliver","last_name":"Dietrich","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gxmswb8nbt","title":"High-resolution genome topology analysis of human peripheral blood mononuclear cells and osteoclasts.","description":"Genome topology analysis of human osteoclasts (Micro-C). In this study we performed Micro-C analysis (Dovetail Genomics) on peripheral blood mononuclear cells and osteoclasts (OC) cultured in vitro, derived from blood samples of 4 healthy human participants. This experiment provided genome topology and chromatin conformation data on these cells to facilitate efficient targeting of ongoing functional genomics studies. We generated a unique high-resolution genome topology dataset for human OC, detecting chromatin loops across the genome. This data should be relevant to studies of the genetics of osteoporosis and to inform future functional studies of osteoclasts.  \n\nPublished as: Wilson SG, Campbell PJ, Sooraj D, Leatherbarrow K, Mullin BH, Brown SJ, Zhu K, Mullin S, Ward BK, Zhang J, Torchia J, Dudbridge F, Xu J, Pavlos NJ, Chandler D, Walsh JP. Genome topology analysis and transcriptomics of human osteoclasts reveals enhancer-promoter interactions at loci for bone traits and diseases. JBMR Plus. 2025 Jul 16;9(10):ziaf120. doi: 10.1093/jbmrpl/ziaf120. PMID: 40918604; PMCID: PMC12409414.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-31T15:39:45.584Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gxmswb8nbt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gxmswb8nbt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Wilson","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/011kf5r70","name":"National Health and Medical Research Council","grant_id":"2021290"}],"categories":["Genomics","Osteoclast","Genome Organization"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hm4ntxztws","title":"The mechanosensitive TRPV4 is required for crystal-induced inflammation ------ TRPV4 mediates crystal-induced inflammation","description":"1. Macrophage-expressed TRPV4 is essential to the generation of crystal-induced joint pain and inflammation and inflammatory responses evoked by diverse crystalline structures.  \n2. TRPV4 is critically involved in the activation of macrophage NLRP3 inflammasome and production of the inflammatory cytokine IL-1 induced by diverse crystalline materials but not non- crystalline NLRP3 inflammasome activators.\n3. A neuroimmune interaction between the TRPV1-expressing primary nociceptors and the TRPV4-expressing synovial macrophages is an important cellular mechanism behind acute gout flares.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-03T07:58:39.422Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hm4ntxztws","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hm4ntxztws"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lvyi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Behavioral Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x8stnrtfrj","title":"Salidroside project","description":"The project tested primarily the influence of salidroside on cytochrome P450 metabolic activities and a possible mechanism of induced changes. The rats were treated with salidroside at the doses from 5 to 45 mg/kg/day for 7 consecutive days. The liver microsomes were isolated and tested for P450 metabolic activities with specific substrates. In vitro inhibition studies with human liver microsomes and naive rat liver microsomes were also performed.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-27T22:54:08.939Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x8stnrtfrj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x8stnrtfrj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Klaskova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Jurica","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Przemyslaw","last_name":"Danek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natalie","last_name":"Mlcuchova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Petra","last_name":"Borilova Linhartova","orcid":"0000-0003-0953-3615"},{"first_name":"Anna Wladyslawa","last_name":"Daniel","orcid":"0000-0002-1226-1721"},{"first_name":"Ondřej","last_name":"Zendulka","orcid":"0000-0001-5038-4899"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/032hzqh22","name":"RECETOX","grant_id":"ELIXIR CZ LM2023055"},{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/501100011659","name":"Lékařská fakulta, Masarykova univerzita","grant_id":"MUNI/IGA/1261/2020"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01p7k1986","name":"Central European Institute of Technology","grant_id":"LM2018132"},{"id":null,"name":"Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Czech republic","grant_id":"MUNI/A/1440/2021"},{"id":null,"name":"Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Czech republic","grant_id":"INFRA CZ LM2018140"},{"id":null,"name":"Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Czech republic","grant_id":"MUNI/A/1342/2022"},{"id":"https://ror.org/032hzqh22","name":"RECETOX","grant_id":"LM2023069"}],"categories":["Pharmacokinetics","Drug Safety","Drug Metabolism through Cytochrome P450"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"p8hw5rt8k4","title":"Salidroside project","description":"The project tested primarily the influence of salidroside on cytochrome P450 metabolic activities and a possible mechanism of induced changes. The rats were treated with salidroside at the doses from 5 to 45 mg/kg/day for 7 consecutive days. The liver microsomes were isolated and tested for P450 metabolic activities with specific substrates. In vitro inhibition studies with human liver microsomes and naive rat liver microsomes were also performed.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-22T10:26:51.935Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/p8hw5rt8k4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/p8hw5rt8k4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Klásková","orcid":"0000-0002-6984-1268"},{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Jurica","orcid":"0000-0002-0257-6711"},{"first_name":"Przemysław Jan ","last_name":"Danek","orcid":"0000-0002-7137-2990"},{"first_name":"Natalie ","last_name":"Mlcuchova","orcid":"0000-0002-5840-1478"},{"first_name":"Saltuk Mustafa ","last_name":"Eyrilmez","orcid":"0000-0003-1650-1961"},{"first_name":"Petra ","last_name":"Borilova Linhartova","orcid":"0000-0003-0953-3615"},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Bednář","orcid":"0000-0002-6803-0340"},{"first_name":"Rajamanikkam ","last_name":"Kamaraj","orcid":"0000-0001-7115-0178"},{"first_name":"Władyslawa Anna ","last_name":"Daniel","orcid":"0000-0002-1226-1721"},{"first_name":"Ondrej","last_name":"Zendulka","orcid":"0000-0001-5038-4899"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/037n8p820","name":"Ministry of Education Youth and Sports","grant_id":"LM2023069"},{"id":"https://ror.org/037n8p820","name":"Ministry of Education Youth and Sports","grant_id":"MUNI/A/1722/2024"},{"id":"https://ror.org/037n8p820","name":"Ministry of Education Youth and Sports","grant_id":"90254"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","grant_id":"LX22NPO5102"},{"id":"https://ror.org/037n8p820","name":"Ministry of Education Youth and Sports","grant_id":"LM2018132"},{"id":"https://ror.org/037n8p820","name":"Ministry of Education Youth and Sports","grant_id":"LM2023055"},{"id":"https://ror.org/02j46qs45","name":"Masaryk University","grant_id":"MUNI/IGA/1261/2020"}],"categories":["Pharmacokinetics","Animal Pharmacology","Cytochrome P450"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h5mwz3w58t","title":"Metagenomic Analysis of  control group chicks","description":"Zearalenone (ZEN), a prevalent mycotoxin produced by fungi of the genus Fusarium, poses significant health risks to animals and humans via contaminated food chains. Despite its recognized toxicity, the transgenerational effects of embryonic egg ZEN exposure on avian development and subsequent chick health remain poorly understood. In this study, embryonic egg injection technology and multi-omics approach were used to investigate the effects of embryonic egg injection of ZEN on hatching performance, post-hatch growth, and physiological health in chicks. A total of 480 Hy-line Brown embryonic eggs were administered varying ZEN doses (0, 5, 50, 500 μg/egg) on day 5 of incubation. ZEN exposure significantly reduced hatchability and initial weight, with dose-dependent decreases in net weight, average daily gain, and average daily feed intake, and an increase in feed conversion ratio at 20 days post-hatch. Histological analysis revealed intestinal inflammation and structural damage, accompanied by gut microbiota dysbiosis, characterized by increased abundance of pathogenic bacteria such as Escherichia coli. ZEN exposure also induced liver damage, evidenced by elevated serum biochemical markers, oxidative stress, and inflammatory responses. Metabolomic profiling indicated significant alterations in hepatic metabolites and metabolic pathways related to lipid metabolism, autophagy, and antioxidant defense. In addition, Correlation analysis suggested that gut microbiota dysbiosis and hepatic metabolic disorders are interconnected, potentially mediating ZEN-induced toxicity via the gut-liver axis. These findings provide novel insights into the mechanisms underlying ZEN's developmental toxicity and highlight the importance of mitigating ZEN contamination in poultry farming.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-05T11:25:56.666Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h5mwz3w58t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h5mwz3w58t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kerui","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pastoral Agriculture"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ncdht53852","title":"Metabolomics analysis of chicks","description":"Zearalenone (ZEN), a prevalent mycotoxin produced by fungi of the genus Fusarium, poses significant health risks to animals and humans via contaminated food chains. Despite its recognized toxicity, the transgenerational effects of embryonic egg ZEN exposure on avian development and subsequent chick health remain poorly understood. In this study, embryonic egg injection technology and multi-omics approach were used to investigate the effects of embryonic egg injection of ZEN on hatching performance, post-hatch growth, and physiological health in chicks. A total of 480 Hy-line Brown embryonic eggs were administered varying ZEN doses (0, 5, 50, 500 μg/egg) on day 5 of incubation. ZEN exposure significantly reduced hatchability and initial weight, with dose-dependent decreases in net weight, average daily gain, and average daily feed intake, and an increase in feed conversion ratio at 20 days post-hatch. Histological analysis revealed intestinal inflammation and structural damage, accompanied by gut microbiota dysbiosis, characterized by increased abundance of pathogenic bacteria such as Escherichia coli. ZEN exposure also induced liver damage, evidenced by elevated serum biochemical markers, oxidative stress, and inflammatory responses. Metabolomic profiling indicated significant alterations in hepatic metabolites and metabolic pathways related to lipid metabolism, autophagy, and antioxidant defense. In addition, Correlation analysis suggested that gut microbiota dysbiosis and hepatic metabolic disorders are interconnected, potentially mediating ZEN-induced toxicity via the gut-liver axis. These findings provide novel insights into the mechanisms underlying ZEN's developmental toxicity and highlight the importance of mitigating ZEN contamination in poultry farming.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-05T08:44:33.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ncdht53852","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ncdht53852"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kerui","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05pwfyy15","name":"National Centre for Research and Development","grant_id":"2023YFD1301004"}],"categories":["Pastoral Agriculture"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":32760,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":149,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":32760,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":32614,"restricted":27,"embargoed":119},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":780},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":264},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":158},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":115},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":115},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":112},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":111},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":108},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","count":85},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":69}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":32264},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":246},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":89},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":42},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":39},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":25},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":16},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":12},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":5},{"id":"db74051a-a8df-4290-8f0e-4f50835cb3a7","name":"University of South Dakota","count":4}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":156},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":107},{"id":"e9576611-ab17-4985-9129-33439e6b617a","name":"IRCCS Ospedale San Raffaele","count":85},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":81},{"id":"7c45f1e4-459e-49af-ae01-e58a59b58646","name":"Binus University","count":79}]}}